/* ============================================================
   速度测试 - 英文文章库（100 篇）
   分级：初级 / 中级 / 高级
   ============================================================ */

const ARTICLES = [
  {"id":1,"level":"初级","title":"The Cat and the Hat","text":"The cat sat on the mat. He had a big red hat. The cat liked to sit in the sun. The sun was hot and the cat was happy. He saw a dog run by. The dog had a long tail. The cat and the dog were good friends. They played in the yard all day. The sun went down and they went home."},
  {"id":2,"level":"初级","title":"A Day at the Park","text":"Tom and his mom went to the park. Tom saw a big tree and a blue pond. He ran to the pond and saw fish in the water. The fish were red and gold. Tom sat on the grass and ate his lunch. A bird came and sat near him. Tom gave the bird some bread. The bird flew away. Tom smiled and went home with his mom."},
  {"id":3,"level":"初级","title":"My Little Dog","text":"I have a little dog. His name is Max. He is brown and white. Max likes to run in the garden. He chases the ball and brings it back to me. Every morning I take Max for a walk. He wags his tail and jumps with joy. Max is my best friend."},
  {"id":4,"level":"初级","title":"The Big Red Bus","text":"The big red bus goes down the street. It stops at the bus stop. People get on and off the bus. The bus driver drives the bus. He takes people to work and school. The bus is long and red. It has big windows. People like to ride the bus."},
  {"id":5,"level":"初级","title":"A Rainy Day","text":"It is raining outside. The rain falls on the roof and the trees. I put on my raincoat and boots. I go outside and jump in the puddles. The rain is cold but fun. I see a rainbow in the sky. It has many colors. The rain stops and the sun comes out again."},
  {"id":6,"level":"初级","title":"The Farm Animals","text":"The farmer has many animals on his farm. The cows eat grass in the field. The chickens lay eggs in the barn. The pigs play in the mud. The horse runs fast in the pasture. The sheep have soft wool. Every morning the farmer feeds all the animals."},
  {"id":7,"level":"初级","title":"My School Day","text":"I wake up at seven o clock. I eat breakfast and brush my teeth. I put on my school uniform and go to school. I study math and English in class. I play with my friends at lunch time. The teacher is nice and helps us learn. I go home at three o clock."},
  {"id":8,"level":"中级","title":"The History of Typing","text":"The first typewriter was invented in the 1860s. It had a keyboard with the letters arranged in alphabetical order. However, the keys often jammed when people typed too fast. To solve this problem, the QWERTY layout was designed. It placed common letters far apart to slow down typing and prevent jams. This layout is still used on most keyboards today, more than one hundred and fifty years later."},
  {"id":9,"level":"中级","title":"Learning to Code","text":"Learning to code is like learning a new language. It takes time and practice. The best way to start is by picking one programming language and sticking with it. Python is a great choice for beginners because it is easy to read and write. You should write small programs every day, even if it is just a few lines. Over time, you will build the skills to create your own projects and solve real-world problems with code."},
  {"id":10,"level":"中级","title":"The Solar System","text":"Our solar system has eight planets. The closest planet to the sun is Mercury. Venus is the hottest planet. Earth is the only planet we know that has life. Mars is called the red planet. Jupiter is the largest planet. Saturn has beautiful rings. Uranus and Neptune are the farthest planets. All the planets orbit the sun in the same direction."},
  {"id":11,"level":"中级","title":"How Bridges Are Built","text":"Bridges are amazing structures that connect two sides of a river or a valley. The first bridges were made of wood or stone. Modern bridges are made of steel and concrete. There are many types of bridges. Beam bridges are simple and strong. Arch bridges use curved supports. Suspension bridges hang from cables. The longest bridge in the world is in China."},
  {"id":12,"level":"中级","title":"The Ocean and Its Creatures","text":"The ocean covers more than seventy percent of our planet. It is home to millions of creatures. The smallest are plankton, which are too tiny to see with the naked eye. The largest is the blue whale, which can grow over thirty meters long. Coral reefs are like underwater cities. They provide food and shelter for many fish. The deep ocean is dark and cold, but life still exists there."},
  {"id":13,"level":"中级","title":"The Story of Paper","text":"Paper was invented in China over two thousand years ago. It was made from plant fibers mixed with water. The mixture was spread thin and dried in the sun. Before paper, people wrote on bamboo or silk. Paper made writing much easier and cheaper. Today, paper is used for books, newspapers, and packaging. Even in the digital age, we still use paper every day."},
  {"id":14,"level":"中级","title":"Why We Dream","text":"Everyone dreams, even if they do not remember it. Dreams happen during a special part of sleep called REM sleep. Some scientists believe dreams help us process our memories and emotions. Others think dreams are the brain way of solving problems. Dreams can be strange and colorful. Sometimes we dream about things that happened during the day. The exact reason for dreaming remains a mystery."},
  {"id":15,"level":"高级","title":"The Future of Artificial Intelligence","text":"Artificial intelligence is transforming the way we live and work. From virtual assistants that answer our questions to self-driving cars that navigate busy streets, AI systems are becoming increasingly capable. However, the rapid development of AI also raises important ethical questions. How do we ensure that AI systems are fair and transparent? How do we protect privacy in a world of intelligent machines? These are challenges that researchers and policymakers must address together."},
  {"id":16,"level":"高级","title":"The Art of Writing Fiction","text":"Fictional writing is a craft that requires both imagination and discipline. A compelling story begins with a strong premise, a situation that captures the reader interest from the very first page. Characters must feel real, with desires and flaws that drive the narrative forward. Dialogue should reveal personality and advance the plot, while description creates a vivid world for the reader to inhabit. The best writers revise their work repeatedly, cutting what is unnecessary and polishing what remains. Writing is rewriting, as the saying goes."},
  {"id":17,"level":"高级","title":"Climate Change and Our Future","text":"Climate change is one of the most pressing challenges facing humanity. The average temperature of the Earth has risen by more than one degree Celsius since the industrial revolution. This warming is caused by greenhouse gases, mainly carbon dioxide from burning fossil fuels. The effects include rising sea levels, more frequent extreme weather events, and loss of biodiversity. To address this crisis, countries around the world are working to reduce emissions and transition to renewable energy sources."},
  {"id":18,"level":"高级","title":"The Psychology of Habit Formation","text":"Habits are automatic behaviors that we perform almost without thinking. They are formed through a loop of cue, routine, and reward. The cue triggers the behavior, the routine is the behavior itself, and the reward is the benefit we get. Understanding this loop is key to changing bad habits and building good ones. Research shows that it takes an average of sixty-six days for a new habit to become automatic, not the twenty-one days that is often claimed. Consistency matters more than intensity."},
  {"id":19,"level":"高级","title":"The History of the Internet","text":"The internet began as a small network of computers in the 1960s. It was called ARPANET and was funded by the US military. The goal was to create a network that could survive a nuclear attack. In the 1990s, the World Wide Web was invented by Tim Berners-Lee. This made the internet accessible to the public. Since then, the internet has grown exponentially. Today, billions of people use the internet for communication, education, entertainment, and commerce. It has changed the way we live."},
  {"id":20,"level":"高级","title":"The Beauty of Classical Music","text":"Classical music has a rich history spanning over four centuries. From the Baroque period of Bach and Handel to the Classical era of Mozart and Haydn, and the Romantic period of Beethoven and Chopin, each era brought new forms and expressions. Symphony orchestras grew from small ensembles to large groups of over a hundred musicians. The piano became the most popular instrument for composition. Classical music continues to influence modern music in film, video games, and popular songs. Its beauty lies in its complexity and emotional depth."},
  {"id":21,"level":"初级","title":"A Sunny Morning","text":"Today the sun is bright and the sky is clear. The birds sing in the tall trees near my house. I open the window and feel the warm air on my face. It is a good day to take a walk. I put on my shoes and go outside. The streets are quiet in the morning. I walk to the small park at the end of the road. The grass is green and the flowers are open. Some children play on the slide while their parents sit on the benches. I find a free seat under a big tree and rest for a while. After a few minutes, I stand up and walk home slowly. A sunny morning is a simple gift, and I am happy to enjoy it."},
  {"id":22,"level":"中级","title":"A Weekend in the Mountains","text":"Last autumn, my friend and I took a bus to a small town in the mountains. The journey took about four hours, and the road twisted between green hills and narrow valleys. When we arrived, the air was cool and smelled of pine. We stayed in a wooden cabin with a fireplace and a small kitchen. Each morning we hiked along rocky trails and stopped to watch the clouds drift over the peaks. In the evening, we cooked simple meals and played cards by the fire. On the final day, a light rain fell, so we visited the local museum and learned about the town's history. We returned home tired but calm. The trip reminded me that a short escape from daily routines can refresh the mind more than a long vacation ever could."},
  {"id":23,"level":"高级","title":"The Silent Archive of Ice","text":"Deep beneath the frozen surface of Greenland and Antarctica lie archives written not in ink but in snow. Each winter deposits a thin layer that records the chemistry of the atmosphere at that moment: dust from distant deserts, ash from ancient volcanoes, bubbles of air trapped since before the industrial age. Glaciologists drill long cylinders of ice and read these layers the way historians read manuscripts. A single core can span hundreds of thousands of years, revealing how climates shifted and how human activity altered the air we breathe. The work is slow and exacting; a sample may be handled for years before its story is told. Yet each measurement sharpens our picture of the planet's past and guides our choices about its future. In this way, the oldest ice becomes a warning, a memory, and a map."},
  {"id":24,"level":"初级","title":"The Friendly Cat Next Door","text":"A gray cat lives next door, and every morning she waits by the fence. When I step outside, she runs to me and rubs her head against my leg. Her name is Mila, and she has soft fur and bright green eyes. I give her a little food and water, and she purrs loudly while she eats. Sometimes she follows me to the garden and sits among the tomato plants. She watches the butterflies and twitches her tail, but she never chases them. In the evening, she climbs onto the roof of the shed and watches the sunset. I think she likes the warm light and the quiet. When I go inside, she blinks slowly at me. That is her way of saying thank you. Having a friendly neighbor like Mila makes each day a little brighter."},
  {"id":25,"level":"中级","title":"The Quiet Power of Walking","text":"Many people believe that staying fit requires a gym membership or a strict diet, but the truth is simpler than they think. Walking, the most natural form of exercise, can improve the body and the mind in remarkable ways. A brisk walk of thirty minutes raises the heart rate, strengthens the legs, and burns extra calories without straining the joints. It also lowers stress: the rhythm of the steps and the fresh air help clear a busy mind. Studies show that people who walk regularly sleep better and feel more energetic during the day. The best part is that walking needs no special equipment and can be done almost anywhere, from a city sidewalk to a forest trail. Start with a short distance and build up slowly. Consistency matters more than speed, and the benefits grow with every step."},
  {"id":26,"level":"高级","title":"Why We Tell Stories","text":"Human beings have told stories for as long as we have had language, and the urge to narrate seems older than writing itself. Around a campfire or a screen, we shape raw experience into beginning, middle, and end, searching for meaning in what feels random. Neuroscience suggests our brains are wired for narrative: we remember events better when they are connected into a tale, and we understand other people more deeply when their lives are presented as journeys rather than lists of facts. Stories cross the boundaries of culture, yet each tradition tells them in its own voice, carrying values and warnings from one generation to the next. A well-told story can make a stranger feel familiar and make a distant time feel close. Storytelling is not entertainment alone; it is how we make sense of being human."},
  {"id":27,"level":"初级","title":"My Morning Routine","text":"Every morning I wake up at the same time, when the light comes through my window. First, I stretch my arms and legs, then I wash my face with cold water. I make a simple breakfast of bread, an egg, and a cup of tea. While I eat, I look out the window at the street below. People walk to work, and a shopkeeper opens his door. After breakfast, I pack my bag and check that I have my keys and my phone. Then I lock the door and walk to the bus stop. The ride takes about twenty minutes, and I enjoy watching the city wake up. By the time I arrive, I feel ready for the day. Small habits make a big difference, and a calm morning sets a good mood for everything that follows."},
  {"id":28,"level":"中级","title":"The Long Distance Between Us","text":"My best friend moved to another city two years ago, and at first I worried that our friendship would slowly fade. We promised to stay in touch, but promises are easy and habits are hard. Then we found a rhythm that works. Every Sunday evening we call each other and talk for an hour about work, family, and small daily news. We send photos of our meals and our walks, and sometimes we watch the same film at the same time, texting our reactions as the scenes unfold. Once a year, we meet in a city halfway between ours and spend a long weekend exploring it together. The distance has not weakened our bond; it has taught us to be deliberate about our time. True friendship survives miles when both people choose to keep the thread alive."},
  {"id":29,"level":"高级","title":"The Age of Small Machines","text":"Modern technology is shrinking at a remarkable pace. The computer that once filled an entire room now fits in a pocket, and researchers are building machines measured in atoms. Nanotechnology, the science of the very small, works with materials a thousand times thinner than a human hair. At that scale, familiar rules bend: gold turns red, carbon becomes stronger than steel, and surfaces change how they repel water. Engineers hope these tiny machines will one day travel through the bloodstream to deliver medicine exactly where it is needed, or sense pollution in a single drop of water. The challenges are enormous, from controlling motion at the molecular level to making such devices safe and affordable. Yet each small step forward brings a world of possibilities. Sometimes the biggest changes come in the smallest packages."},
  {"id":30,"level":"初级","title":"A Simple Bowl of Soup","text":"My grandmother makes the best soup in the world, and her secret is patience. She starts with onions and garlic, cooking them slowly in a little oil until the kitchen fills with a sweet smell. Then she adds carrots, potatoes, and water, and lets the pot simmer for a long time on a low flame. She says the soup cannot be rushed; every ingredient needs time to share its flavor. When it is ready, she serves it in big bowls with warm bread on the side. The first spoonful is always the best: hot, smooth, and full of small pieces of soft vegetables. We eat together at the table, talking and laughing between bites. On cold days, that simple bowl of soup feels like a warm hug. Good food, she tells me, is made with love and time."},
  {"id":31,"level":"中级","title":"The Four Seasons of a Small Town","text":"In the small town where I grew up, the weather was never quiet. Spring arrived with soft rain that turned the streets silver, and the air smelled of wet earth and fresh grass. Summer came next, bringing long hot days when children ran through sprinklers and old men sat under the elm trees with newspapers over their faces. Autumn was the best season, when the maple leaves turned red and gold and covered the sidewalks like a warm carpet. Then winter swept in with sharp wind and snow that piled against the doors. The people did not complain. They simply changed their coats, lit their stoves, and waited for spring to return once more, because they knew the cycle would never fail them."},
  {"id":32,"level":"高级","title":"The Quiet Revolution of the Printing Press","text":"The invention of the printing press in the fifteenth century did not merely speed up the copying of books; it quietly transformed the structure of European society. Before movable type, knowledge belonged to the few who could afford handwritten manuscripts, and ideas traveled at the pace of a horse. Once presses began to roll, pamphlets, maps, and scientific treatises spread across borders within weeks. Scholars could compare conflicting accounts, merchants could share reliable numbers, and ordinary readers gained access to arguments that once reached only the cloister. Some rulers feared this flood of words and banned certain texts, yet censorship only sharpened public curiosity. Within a few generations, the habit of questioning authority became a common skill, not a rare privilege. The press did not invent doubt, but it gave doubt a voice loud enough to be heard by everyone."},
  {"id":33,"level":"初级","title":"A Morning Walk","text":"I like to walk in the park every morning. The air is cool and clean. Birds sing in the tall trees, and small dogs run on the grass with their owners. I pass a bench where an old man feeds the pigeons. He smiles at me, and I smile back. Then I stop by the small lake. The water is quiet, and the sun makes it shine like a mirror. I watch the ducks swim in a line. After a short rest, I walk home. I buy bread and milk at the corner shop. When I get back, I make tea and sit by the window. The morning walk is my favorite part of the day. It makes me feel calm and ready for work."},
  {"id":34,"level":"中级","title":"A Dog Named Max","text":"Max came into our lives on a rainy Tuesday, a thin brown dog with one torn ear and eyes that seemed older than his body. My son Ben was nine then, and he had been asking for a dog for years. We found Max at the shelter, shaking in the back of his cage, and Ben sat on the floor beside him for an hour until the dog finally crept forward and rested his head on the boy's knee. From that day they were inseparable. They walked to the bus stop together, shared afternoon snacks, and slept curled at the foot of the bed. Max taught Ben patience and kindness, and Ben gave Max a second chance at a happy life. Watching them grow up, I understood that friendship needs no words, only trust that builds slowly."},
  {"id":35,"level":"高级","title":"The Museum of Small Things","text":"Every city has a grand museum, but the one I love best is a narrow shop that calls itself the Museum of Small Things. Behind its dusty window sit a brass compass from a ship that never returned, a bundle of love letters tied with red ribbon, and a wooden shoe carved by a soldier in his spare time. What makes these relics precious is not their value but their silence. Each one holds a life that ended long ago, and each one asks the same question: who held me, and what were they thinking? To walk through such a place is to practice humility. It reminds us that history is not a list of famous names but a vast ocean of ordinary days, where every forgotten object was once the center of someone's world."},
  {"id":36,"level":"初级","title":"My Grandmother's Kitchen","text":"I love my grandmother's kitchen. It is small and warm, and it always smells of fresh bread and cinnamon. Every Sunday she cooks dinner for the whole family. She makes soup with vegetables from her garden, roast chicken with golden skin, and soft potatoes with butter. While the food cooks, we sit at the table and talk. She tells us stories about her childhood, and we laugh at her funny jokes. The best part comes at the end, when she brings out her apple pie. The crust is sweet, and the filling is warm and juicy. We eat slowly and share the last piece. After dinner we wash the dishes together and sing old songs. I think the food tastes so good because it is made with love, and love is the best secret ingredient of all."},
  {"id":37,"level":"中级","title":"The Train Through the Mountains","text":"There is a railway that winds through the high mountains, and I have ridden it three times in my life, always in winter. The train is old, with wooden seats and windows that frost over in the cold. As it climbs, the view changes slowly: first pine forests, then bare rock, then snow that covers everything like a white blanket. At the highest stop, the conductor lets passengers step out for five minutes. The silence there is complete. You can hear your own heartbeat, and the air is so thin and clear that the peaks seem close enough to touch. Then the whistle blows, and the train creeps onward through tunnels carved a hundred years ago. I have flown across oceans and driven across deserts, but no journey has ever felt more like an adventure than that slow, patient climb into the clouds."},
  {"id":38,"level":"高级","title":"The Hidden Lives of Trees","text":"Modern science has revealed that a forest is not a collection of separate trees but a living network. Beneath the soil, roots are joined by a fine web of fungi that scientists call the wood wide web. Through this network, trees exchange water, nutrients, and chemical signals. When one tree is attacked by insects, it sends a warning that travels through the fungal threads, allowing its neighbors to raise their defenses in time. Old trees act as mother trees, feeding young seedlings that grow in their shade and keeping the whole community stable. This discovery has changed how we think about forests and about competition itself. A forest, it turns out, is less like a crowd of rivals and more like a family that shares what it has, quietly and without complaint, for centuries on end."},
  {"id":39,"level":"初级","title":"My Little Garden","text":"I have a small garden behind my house. Last spring, I decided to grow vegetables for the first time. I bought seeds for tomatoes, carrots, and green beans. At first, nothing happened, and I felt sad. Then one morning, I saw tiny green leaves pushing out of the soil. I was so happy that I called my mother to tell her. Every day after work, I water the plants and pull out the weeds. The tomatoes grow slowly, turning from green to red. In summer, I picked my first ripe tomato and ate it right there in the garden. It was the sweetest thing I had ever tasted. Now I plant something new every season. Gardening has taught me patience, and it gives me fresh food and quiet joy all year long."},
  {"id":40,"level":"中级","title":"The First Day at a New Job","text":"Starting a new job is always a little frightening. On my first morning, I walked into a bright office where everyone seemed to know exactly what to do. A kind woman named Rosa showed me to my desk and explained how the coffee machine worked. Then she introduced me to the team, one by one, and I tried to remember every name. The work itself was not hard, but I made small mistakes and felt my face turn red. Rosa just laughed and said that everyone makes mistakes in the first week. By lunchtime, I had learned to use the printer, and by the end of the day, I had finished my first real task. On the way home, I felt tired but proud. Starting something new is never easy, but with patient people around you, it gets easier every day."},
  {"id":41,"level":"高级","title":"The Night Sky","text":"The night sky has fascinated people since the first humans looked up and wondered. Ancient stargazers mapped the stars without telescopes, tracking their slow journey across the seasons. They built observatories from stone and told stories about the constellations that still shape our culture today. Modern astronomers use powerful instruments to study galaxies that lie millions of light years away, yet the same questions remain. How did the universe begin? Are we alone? Every clear evening offers a reminder that wonder does not require expensive equipment. A pair of eyes, a dark place, and a little patience are enough to begin a lifelong journey of discovery."},
  {"id":42,"level":"初级","title":"My Morning","text":"Every morning I wake up early. The sun comes through my window and I know it is time to get up. I wash my face and brush my teeth. Then I eat a simple breakfast of bread and milk. After that, I put on my clothes and take my bag. I walk to the bus stop near my house. The bus comes at seven. I sit by the window and watch the streets wake up. People walk their dogs. Shops open their doors. I like this quiet time of day. It helps me feel ready for school and all the things I need to do."},
  {"id":43,"level":"中级","title":"Why We Travel","text":"Travel changes the way we see the world, but it also changes the way we see ourselves. When you visit a new city, every street feels like a question waiting for an answer. You learn to read signs in unfamiliar languages, to order food without knowing the names of the dishes, and to find your way home when the map disagrees with you. These small challenges build confidence. You discover that strangers are often kinder than you expected, and that a shared smile needs no translation. The best journeys are not the ones with perfect plans. They are the ones where you leave room for surprise, because the most memorable moments rarely appear on any itinerary."},
  {"id":44,"level":"高级","title":"The Quiet Power of Libraries","text":"Libraries are among the oldest institutions of learning, and their quiet rooms still hold the keys to our shared history. Before printing presses spread across Europe, scribes copied every book by hand, and a single volume could take a year to finish. That painstaking labor made each manuscript precious, which is why so much care went into their decoration and preservation. When printed books finally became common, knowledge moved faster than ever before. Yet the library itself never lost its purpose. It remains a place where anyone can sit down and meet the greatest minds of every century, one page at a time, free of charge and full of wonder."},
  {"id":45,"level":"初级","title":"My Little Cat","text":"My cat is small and gray. She likes to sleep on the sofa all day. When I come home from school, she runs to the door and meows at me. I think she is saying hello. In the evening we play with a little ball. She jumps and runs after it. Sometimes she stops and looks at me, as if she wants me to throw it again. At night she curls up on my bed and purrs. The sound is soft and warm. Having a pet is a lot of fun. She makes me laugh, and she makes the house feel like a home."},
  {"id":46,"level":"中级","title":"The Value of Sleep","text":"Sleep is not a waste of time, no matter how busy you feel. During the night your body repairs tired muscles, your brain sorts through the day's memories, and your mood resets for the morning. People who sleep poorly often struggle to focus, feel hungry more easily, and catch colds more quickly. The fix does not have to be complicated. Try to keep a regular bedtime, dim the lights an hour before you rest, and put your phone away. A quiet, cool room helps too. Most adults need about seven to nine hours each night. If you treat sleep as a priority instead of an interruption, you may find that everything else becomes a little easier."},
  {"id":47,"level":"高级","title":"The Hidden Ocean","text":"The ocean covers most of our planet, yet we have explored less of its depths than we have of the moon's surface. Sunlight fades quickly beneath the waves, and beyond a few hundred meters the world turns cold and dark. Strange creatures live there, animals that make their own light and survive on the slow rain of food from above. Studying these deep waters is difficult and costly, but every dive reveals something new. The ocean also shapes our weather, stores heat from the sun, and produces much of the air we breathe. Protecting it is not only about saving whales and coral reefs. It is about protecting the conditions that make life possible for everyone."},
  {"id":48,"level":"初级","title":"Cooking at Home","text":"I like to cook simple food. My favorite dish is fried rice. First I cut some vegetables into small pieces. I use carrots and green beans. Then I put a little oil in the pan and cook the vegetables until they are soft. Next I add the rice and mix everything together. I put in some salt and a few drops of soy sauce. In five minutes, my dinner is ready. The best part is the smell. When my family smells the food, they come to the kitchen and ask what I made. Cooking is easy and fun, and the food always tastes better when you make it yourself."},
  {"id":49,"level":"中级","title":"Growing Friendships","text":"Good friendships do not appear overnight. They grow slowly, like a tree that needs years of sunlight and rain. You meet someone at school or at work, and at first you only talk about small things. Then one day you share a real story, and the other person listens without judging. That is the moment a friendship begins to deepen. Real friends celebrate your wins and stay close during hard times. They tell you the truth, even when it is not easy to hear. In a busy world, friendship asks for time and attention. But the reward is simple: someone who knows your story and still chooses to walk beside you."},
  {"id":50,"level":"高级","title":"The Changing Seasons","text":"Every season writes its own story across the land. Spring arrives with soft rains and sudden green, when the first warm days feel like a promise kept. Summer brings long evenings, heavy heat, and the smell of cut grass. Autumn paints the trees in gold and red before the wind strips them bare. Winter silences the world under frost and snow, and the short days teach us to value warmth. People often complain about the weather, yet it shapes our food, our clothes, our holidays, and even our mood. Learning to notice the small changes between seasons is a quiet pleasure that costs nothing and enriches every ordinary day."},
  {"id":51,"level":"初级","title":"Spring Is a Gift","text":"Spring is a lovely season. The air becomes warm and soft. Little flowers push up from the wet ground, and the trees grow new green leaves. Birds sing in the morning, and the sun shines longer each day. People take off their heavy coats and go for walks in the park. Children fly kites in the open fields. The rain falls gently, and the world smells fresh and clean. After a long winter, spring feels like a gift. It reminds us that change can be beautiful. When you step outside, take a slow breath and enjoy the simple joy of a new day."},
  {"id":52,"level":"中级","title":"Walking Through Barcelona","text":"Barcelona is a city that seems to hum with life at every hour. Wide boulevards sweep down toward the sea, while narrow lanes twist through old quarters full of tiny cafes and busy markets. The buildings tell stories of many ages, from ancient walls to the strange, curving towers designed by Gaudi. Locals greet each other with warm hellos, and the smell of fresh bread and coffee drifts from open doors. Visitors often say that the best way to see the city is simply to walk without a plan. Every corner holds a surprise, whether it is a hidden courtyard, a street musician, or a shop selling handmade tiles. Barcelona rewards the curious traveler who takes time to look."},
  {"id":53,"level":"高级","title":"The Quiet Engine of Life","text":"Photosynthesis is the quiet engine that powers nearly all life on Earth. Inside the leaves of green plants, tiny structures called chloroplasts capture sunlight and use its energy to combine water and carbon dioxide into sugars. Those sugars become the food that plants grow on, and they pass along the food chain to animals and to us. As a byproduct, the process releases oxygen into the air, the very gas that most living creatures need to breathe. Scientists study this process with great care, because a deeper understanding may help us grow more food with less water and even design cleaner fuels. In a sense, every breath we take is a gift from a leaf, and every meal traces back to a sunbeam."},
  {"id":54,"level":"初级","title":"My Clever Cat","text":"My cat is small and very clever. She knows the sound of the food bag from across the house, and she runs to the kitchen the moment she hears it. In the morning she sits by my bed and waits for breakfast. She likes fish and soft chicken, but she never touches vegetables. After eating, she cleans her paws and then finds a sunny spot for a long nap. In the evening she plays with a little ball until she is tired. I think she is the best friend a person could have. She does not speak, but she always knows how I feel, and she stays close when I am sad. Home is much warmer with a cat in it."},
  {"id":55,"level":"中级","title":"Move Your Body, Clear Your Mind","text":"Regular exercise does far more than shape the body; it shapes the mind as well. When we move, the heart pumps faster and sends fresh blood to the brain, which helps us think clearly and remember more easily. Exercise also releases natural chemicals that lift the mood and ease worry, which is why a brisk walk often feels like a reset button for a stressful day. You do not need a gym or fancy equipment to start. A short jog in the park, a few stretches beside the bed, or a dance in the living room all count. The secret is consistency, not intensity. Small daily habits build into real change over time, and the best routine is simply the one you can keep."},
  {"id":56,"level":"高级","title":"The Silk Road","text":"The Silk Road was never a single road but a shifting web of trade routes that linked China with the distant markets of Central Asia, Persia, and Europe. For more than a thousand years, caravans of camels carried silk, spices, glass, and paper across deserts and mountain passes, while ideas traveled in the opposite direction along with the goods. Buddhism spread eastward along these routes, and new fruits and crops moved westward, changing what people ate on both continents. The exchanges were rarely simple or peaceful; empires rose and fell, and bandits lurked along the loneliest stretches. Yet the network endured because people on every side found value in what others had to offer. Trade, it turns out, is one of the oldest forms of conversation between cultures."},
  {"id":57,"level":"初级","title":"My Evening Reading","text":"Every evening after dinner, I sit by the window and read a book. I like quiet stories about people who live far away or long ago. The lamp is warm, and my cup of tea is hot. Sometimes I read just one page, and sometimes I read a whole chapter. The noise of the day fades, and my mind slows down. Reading is my favorite way to rest, because a good book takes me to another world without leaving my chair. On weekends I also write in a small notebook about what I saw and felt. Little habits like these make ordinary days feel rich and full."},
  {"id":58,"level":"中级","title":"Friends at Work","text":"Good friendships at work can change the way a job feels from day to day. When you share a desk with someone who understands your jokes and your worries, even a heavy deadline seems lighter. Small gestures matter most: a cup of coffee left on a desk, a quick message of encouragement before a big meeting, or an honest word of advice after a mistake. These bonds grow slowly, through shared lunches and late afternoons, and they should not be forced. The best work friendships feel natural, built on trust rather than on office politics. A team that laughs together also solves problems together, because people speak freely when they are not afraid. In the end, we remember colleagues less for the tasks we finished and more for the kindness we shared along the way."},
  {"id":59,"level":"高级","title":"Wisdom Over Speed","text":"Artificial intelligence has moved from science fiction into daily life faster than most people expected. It helps doctors read scans, helps farmers decide when to water their fields, and helps drivers avoid accidents on busy roads. Yet the same technology raises difficult questions about fairness and privacy. A system trained on old data may repeat old biases, and a machine that learns our habits may also learn our secrets. Engineers argue that the answer lies in design: algorithms should be transparent, data should be handled with care, and human judgment should keep the final word. The future of this tool depends less on what it can do and more on the rules we choose to guide it. Progress without wisdom is only speed in the wrong direction."},
  {"id":60,"level":"初级","title":"A Rainy Day at Home","text":"Today the sky is gray and the wind is cool, so I stay home and do small things. I make breakfast slowly and listen to the rain tap on the window. The house feels quiet and safe. I sweep the floor, water the plants, and bake a simple cake. The kitchen smells sweet, and the warm oven fights the cold air outside. In the afternoon the rain stops, and a little light breaks through the clouds. I put on my coat and walk to the corner shop for milk. The streets are clean and empty. On the way back, I see a rainbow above the roofs. A rainy day is never wasted if you fill it with gentle, happy work."},
  {"id":61,"level":"中级","title":"The Science of Rainbows","text":"A rainbow appears when sunlight passes through raindrops in the sky. Each tiny drop bends the light and splits it into colors, from red at the top to violet at the bottom. You usually see a rainbow after a storm, when the sun shines while the rain is still falling. To see one, stand with your back to the sun and look toward the shower. The arc seems to touch the ground, but it always moves away as you walk toward it. People have told stories about pots of gold at the end of the rainbow, yet no one has ever found one. The truth is just as wonderful: a rainbow is a trick of light, free for anyone who knows where to look."},
  {"id":62,"level":"高级","title":"The Silk Road","text":"For more than a thousand years, the Silk Road was the busiest trade route on Earth. It was not a single road but a web of paths that linked China with the markets of Central Asia, Persia, and the Mediterranean. Caravans of camels carried silk, spices, and porcelain westward, while gold, glass, and horses traveled east. The journey took months and demanded great courage, for the route crossed deserts, mountains, and lands ruled by many different kings. Along the way, merchants exchanged not only goods but also ideas, religions, and inventions. The printing press, the compass, and paper all moved across these trails. When the sea routes opened, the old roads faded, but their legacy shaped the modern world in ways we still feel today."},
  {"id":63,"level":"初级","title":"My Lazy Cat","text":"My cat is a very lazy animal. She sleeps all day in the sun and wakes up only when it is time to eat. In the morning, she sits by the window and watches the birds. She moves her tail slowly and makes a soft sound. Sometimes she chases a red ball across the floor, but she gets tired fast. At night, she likes to lie on my bed and purr. I like to pet her soft fur and talk to her. She is my best friend, even though she never listens to me. I think she understands me anyway."},
  {"id":64,"level":"中级","title":"The Joy of Train Travel","text":"There is something special about traveling by train. Unlike a plane, a train lets you watch the world move past your window at a gentle pace. You can see small towns, green fields, and rivers that you would never notice from the sky. Many people use the time to read, to work, or simply to daydream. The rocking motion of the carriage is strangely calming, and the sound of the wheels on the track becomes a steady rhythm. When you arrive, you feel that the journey itself was part of the adventure, not just a way to reach a destination. Next time you plan a trip, consider the railway. You may find that getting there is half the joy."},
  {"id":65,"level":"高级","title":"Why Sleep Matters","text":"Sleep is not a luxury; it is a biological necessity. During the night, the brain sorts through the day's memories, deciding what to keep and what to discard. Muscles repair themselves, and the immune system grows stronger. Yet modern life treats sleep as an obstacle to productivity. People brag about sleeping only five hours, as if rest were a sign of weakness. The evidence tells a different story. Chronic lack of sleep impairs judgment, weakens memory, and increases the risk of serious illness. It also dulls the emotions, making small problems feel enormous. The fix is simple but hard to follow: keep a regular schedule, dim the lights before bed, and put the phone away. Good sleep is not wasted time. It is the foundation upon which every waking hour is built."},
  {"id":66,"level":"初级","title":"Simple Fried Rice","text":"I love to cook simple food. My favorite dish is fried rice. First, I cut some vegetables into small pieces. I use carrots, onions, and green peas. Then I heat oil in a pan and cook the vegetables for a few minutes. Next, I add cold rice and stir everything together. I put in a little salt and some soy sauce. After that, I push the rice to one side and cook an egg in the pan. When the egg is ready, I mix it all up. The whole house smells wonderful. I eat my fried rice with a spoon and a cup of tea. It is easy, fast, and delicious."},
  {"id":67,"level":"中级","title":"The Garden of Friendship","text":"True friendship is built slowly, the way a garden grows. It begins with small moments: a shared lunch, a walk after school, a joke that only the two of you understand. Over time, these moments become trust. A real friend listens without judging and speaks without hurting. They celebrate your success as if it were their own and stand beside you when things go wrong. Friendships do not need constant contact to survive. You can go months without a phone call, yet pick up right where you left off. What matters is not how often you meet, but how you feel when you do. A good friend makes you feel seen, heard, and understood. If you have such a person in your life, treasure them, because true friends are rare."},
  {"id":68,"level":"高级","title":"The Soul of a City","text":"Every great city has a personality of its own, shaped by history, geography, and the people who live there. Some cities feel like machines, always moving, their streets crowded with commuters who never look up. Others feel like villages, where shopkeepers know their customers by name and the pace of life is unhurried. The best cities are not the ones with the tallest buildings, but the ones that reward wandering. A hidden courtyard, a quiet cafe, a market that has stood for centuries: these are the places that give a city its soul. Visitors often chase famous landmarks and miss what matters. The true character of a city is found in its ordinary corners, in the smell of bread in the morning and the sound of laughter in the evening. Cities change, but the memories stay forever."},
  {"id":69,"level":"初级","title":"Whatever the Weather","text":"The weather changes every day, and I always check the sky in the morning. If the clouds are gray and low, I take my umbrella. If the sun is bright, I wear a hat and go out to play. Rainy days are my favorite. I like the sound of rain on the roof and the smell of wet earth. After the rain, the air feels clean and fresh. Sometimes I see a rainbow in the sky. On windy days, I fly my kite in the park. Snow days are the best of all. I build a snowman with my friends and throw snowballs until my hands get cold. Then I go inside and drink hot chocolate. No matter what the weather brings, there is always something fun to do."},
  {"id":70,"level":"中级","title":"Learning to Paint","text":"Learning a new hobby can change the way you see the world. Last year, I started painting with watercolors. At first, my pictures looked like messy blobs of color, and I almost gave up. But I kept practicing a little every weekend. I watched videos, read books, and copied the work of painters I admired. Slowly, my hands learned what my eyes could see. Now I notice colors everywhere: the purple shadow under a tree, the orange glow of a street lamp, the blue of the sky after rain. My hobby has taught me patience and the joy of small progress. You do not need talent to begin. You only need curiosity and the willingness to make mistakes. That is how every skill, and every artist, begins."},
  {"id":71,"level":"高级","title":"The Quiet Machine That Changed the World","text":"Before the printing press, books were copied by hand, and a single volume could take months to finish. Scribes worked in quiet rooms, their hands moving slowly across parchment, and errors multiplied with every retelling. When movable type arrived, the change was sudden and profound. Texts could be produced in days, not seasons, and ideas began to travel faster than armies. Scholars compared notes across borders, ordinary readers gained access to scripture and science, and literacy slowly spread through towns and cities. The press did not merely copy words; it reshaped thought itself. Authorities who once controlled knowledge through scarcity found themselves struggling to contain a flood of printed pages. Looking back, we can see that this quiet machine lit the fuse for revolutions in religion, politics, and education that still echo in our own age."},
  {"id":72,"level":"初级","title":"My Little Dog Max","text":"I have a small dog. His name is Max. Max is brown and white, and his ears are soft. Every morning, Max wakes me up by licking my face. He likes to run in the park and catch a red ball. When I eat, he sits beside me and looks at my food with big eyes. At night, Max sleeps on a blue blanket near my bed. He is my best friend. I love Max very much."},
  {"id":73,"level":"中级","title":"A Weekend by the Sea","text":"Last spring, I spent a weekend in a small coastal town that I had only seen in photographs. The train ride took three hours, and the last part ran beside the sea, where the tracks curved close to the water. The town itself was quiet and colorful, with narrow streets, white houses, and shops selling fresh bread and handmade pottery. I walked along the harbor in the evening, watching the fishing boats bob on the tide while seagulls circled overhead. On the second day, I climbed the hill behind the town and looked out over the rooftops to the open ocean. It was a short trip, but it reminded me how much joy a change of scenery can bring. I already plan to return next year."},
  {"id":74,"level":"高级","title":"The Hidden Work of Sleep","text":"Sleep is not a passive pause in our day; it is an active and essential process. While we rest, the brain replays the events we lived through, sorting memories into lasting storage and discarding the noise. Deep sleep allows the body to repair tissue, refresh the immune system, and balance the hormones that govern appetite and mood. Yet modern life treats sleep as a luxury rather than a necessity. Screens glow late into the night, schedules stretch past midnight, and caffeine papers over the damage. The consequences are subtle at first, a slower thought, a shorter temper, but they compound over years. Scientists who study sleep are increasingly convinced that its quality shapes our health as much as diet and exercise. Protecting our rest, they argue, is one of the simplest and most powerful gifts we can give ourselves."},
  {"id":75,"level":"初级","title":"A Rainy Day","text":"It is raining today. The rain falls on the roof and makes a soft sound. I like to watch the drops on the window. Some drops are big, and some are small. The street is wet, and the cars make little waves. After the rain, the air smells fresh and clean. The sky turns blue again, and a rainbow may appear. I put on my boots and jump in the puddles. It is fun. Rain helps the flowers grow, and I am happy to see them bloom. Rain is a good friend to the earth."},
  {"id":76,"level":"中级","title":"A Door to Another World","text":"Learning a new language is like opening a door to another world. At first, the words feel strange, and the grammar seems full of traps. I remember my first month of studying English: every sentence was a puzzle, and I often mixed up the order of words. Slowly, things began to change. I listened to songs and repeated the lines until they felt natural. I watched films with simple dialogues and wrote down new phrases in a small notebook. The real breakthrough came when I met a friend from another country. We spoke slowly at first, laughing at our mistakes, and bit by bit we understood each other. Language is not just rules; it is a bridge between people. Every new word I learn brings me closer to someone I might never have met."},
  {"id":77,"level":"高级","title":"The Living Archive","text":"A library is more than a building full of books; it is a living archive of human curiosity. Within its quiet rooms, a farmer can learn astronomy, a child can travel to ancient kingdoms, and a tired worker can find a novel that changes how they see the world. For centuries, libraries have guarded the fragile memory of our species. Scrolls survived fires, manuscripts outlasted empires, and now digital collections stretch across continents in an instant. Yet the oldest purpose remains unchanged: to connect a curious mind with the knowledge it seeks. Walking between the shelves, you feel the weight of all the questions people have asked, and the patience of all the answers waiting to be found. In an age of noise, the library is still a place where silence speaks most clearly."},
  {"id":78,"level":"初级","title":"My Morning Breakfast","text":"Breakfast is my favorite meal of the day. In the morning, I wake up hungry. I go to the kitchen and make a simple breakfast. I boil an egg and toast a slice of bread. I pour a glass of milk and cut a banana into small pieces. The kitchen smells warm and nice. I sit by the window and eat slowly. Outside, birds sing in the trees. After breakfast, I wash my cup and plate. Then I brush my teeth, pack my bag, and go to school. A good breakfast gives me energy for the whole morning. I never skip it, because it makes my day start right."},
  {"id":79,"level":"中级","title":"Patience in the Garden","text":"Gardening taught me patience in a way no other hobby could. You cannot hurry a seed; you can only prepare the soil, water it, and wait. My small vegetable patch began as a patch of bare earth behind the house. I spent a whole weekend turning the ground and pulling out stones and old roots. Then came the seeds, tiny and unimpressive, buried an inch deep in neat rows. For weeks, nothing seemed to happen, and I checked the garden every morning with fading hope. Then one day, green shoots appeared, thin and fragile, pushing through the dark soil. From that moment, the garden took on a life of its own. Tomatoes, beans, and lettuce grew week by week. Harvesting my own vegetables felt like a quiet victory, and every meal tasted better for it."},
  {"id":80,"level":"高级","title":"Reading the History of the Universe","text":"Every star we see at night is a distant sun, and many host worlds of their own. Astronomers spend their lives asking a simple question: are we alone? With each generation of telescopes, the answer draws closer. Early observers mapped the sky with the naked eye; then glass lenses revealed the moons of other planets and the slow dance of distant galaxies. Today, powerful instruments catch the faint light of worlds orbiting stars far beyond our own, studying their air for signs of life. The search is slow and full of silence, but it is one of the boldest projects humanity has ever undertaken. When you look up on a clear night, the light that reaches your eyes left its source long ago. We are not just watching the stars; we are reading the history of the universe."},
  {"id":81,"level":"初级","title":"Morning Weather","text":"Every morning I look out the window before I get dressed. If the sky is blue and the sun is out, I feel happy. I put on light clothes and walk to the park. The birds sing in the trees, and the air smells fresh. On rainy days, I stay home and read a book by the window. I like the sound of rain on the roof. It makes the room feel cozy and warm. After the rain stops, the world looks clean and new. I love how the weather changes each day. It keeps every morning a little different."},
  {"id":82,"level":"中级","title":"A Walk in the Old Forest","text":"Last weekend I joined a small group of friends for a hike through the old forest near the river. The trail was narrow and winding, covered with fallen leaves that rustled under our boots. Tall pines blocked most of the sunlight, so the air stayed cool even at noon. We saw a deer standing still among the ferns, watching us with wide, calm eyes. Nobody spoke for a moment. Further along, the path opened into a clearing where wildflowers grew in yellow and purple clusters. We stopped there for lunch, listening to the stream that ran beside us. On the way back, the sunset painted the treetops gold. It was a quiet day, and I felt my mind slow down with every step."},
  {"id":83,"level":"高级","title":"The Journey of a Drop of Water","text":"Every drop of water on Earth has been cycling through the same grand circuit for millions of years. Sunlight warms the oceans, driving evaporation that lifts countless tons of vapor into the sky. As the vapor rises, it cools and condenses around microscopic particles of dust, forming clouds that drift across continents. When the droplets grow heavy enough, they fall as rain or snow, soaking into soil, feeding rivers, and returning to the sea. Yet the cycle is anything but uniform. Climate shapes how fast it spins: deserts recycle moisture slowly, while tropical storms can move vast quantities in a single afternoon. Human activity now nudges the system in measurable ways, altering rainfall patterns and intensifying droughts. Understanding this delicate machinery matters, because every glass of water you drink once passed through clouds, rivers, and the sea."},
  {"id":84,"level":"初级","title":"My Cat Mittens","text":"My cat Mittens is the boss of our house, and she knows it. Every morning she sits on the kitchen table until I pour her breakfast. After eating, she follows me from room to room, purring like a tiny engine. She loves the sunny spot on the living room rug, where she naps for hours with one ear twitching. When I come home from school, she runs to the door and weaves between my legs. At night she curls up on my bed and kneads the blanket with her soft paws. She never speaks a word, but I always know what she wants. A look, a meow, a flick of her tail, and I understand her perfectly."},
  {"id":85,"level":"中级","title":"A Train Trip Along the Coast","text":"Last summer my family took a train trip along the coast, and it became my favorite memory of the year. We woke before sunrise, packed simple bags, and boarded a quiet train that smelled of coffee and sea air. The windows showed a changing picture all day long: rocky cliffs, sandy beaches, small fishing villages, and lighthouses standing on the edge of the water. At one station we jumped off for an hour and bought fresh bread and cheese from a market by the tracks. The train carried us on, and by evening we reached a port town where the boats bobbed in orange light. We ate dinner at a tiny restaurant above the harbor, watching the waves roll in. I learned that the journey itself can be the best part of a holiday."},
  {"id":86,"level":"高级","title":"The Silk Road","text":"The Silk Road was never a single road but a web of trade routes that linked China with the Mediterranean world for more than a thousand years. Caravans of camels carried silk, spices, and precious stones across deserts and mountain passes, trading them for glass, wool, and gold. Yet goods were only part of the story. Ideas traveled with the merchants: papermaking techniques moved westward, while Buddhism and later Islam spread along the same paths. Cities like Samarkand grew wealthy as meeting points where languages, religions, and cuisines mixed freely. The network also carried disease, and historians argue that plagues sometimes followed the caravans. When sea routes opened in the fifteenth century, the old land paths slowly lost their importance. Still, the exchange of knowledge that flowed along them reshaped entire civilizations on both ends of the journey."},
  {"id":87,"level":"初级","title":"Sunday Pancakes","text":"Sunday breakfast is my favorite meal of the week because my dad makes pancakes. First we mix flour, milk, and eggs in a big blue bowl. Dad pours the batter onto the hot pan, and tiny bubbles appear on top. When the edges turn golden, he flips each pancake high into the air. My sister and I cheer every time he catches it. We stack the pancakes on a plate, then add butter and a little honey. The honey runs down the sides like a golden river. We sit at the table with steaming cups of tea and talk about the week ahead. The kitchen smells sweet, and everyone smiles. It is the perfect way to start a lazy Sunday."},
  {"id":88,"level":"中级","title":"The Quiet Work of Sleep","text":"Sleep is the quiet worker that repairs our bodies while we dream. Scientists say most adults need about seven to nine hours each night, yet many of us fall short and pay for it the next day. Poor sleep makes it harder to focus, weakens the immune system, and leaves us irritable by afternoon. The good news is that small habits can help. Going to bed at the same time each night trains the body clock. Keeping screens out of the bedroom reduces the blue light that tricks the brain into staying awake. A short walk in the evening and a warm bath can also signal that the day is over. If you wake feeling tired every morning, try changing one habit at a time. Rest is not laziness; it is part of staying healthy."},
  {"id":89,"level":"高级","title":"How Cities Breathe","text":"Modern cities are living machines that breathe with the rhythm of their residents. Every morning millions of commuters pour through stations and tunnels, and every evening the same networks carry them home again. The layout of a city shapes this daily dance in surprising ways. Wide boulevards speed traffic but can divide neighborhoods, while narrow, winding streets slow cars and encourage walking. Parks and rivers give the concrete a chance to cool down on hot days. Good public transport can shrink a city, bringing jobs closer to homes and cutting the hours people spend in transit. Planners now study how buildings, light, and green space affect mood and health. A city that works well feels effortless: you never notice the systems, only the freedom to move. That invisible success is the true art of urban design."},
  {"id":90,"level":"初级","title":"My Best Friend Leo","text":"My best friend Leo moved to our street last spring, and now we do almost everything together. On school days we walk to the bus stop and share our snacks. After class we play soccer in the park until the streetlights come on. Leo is good at drawing, and he teaches me new tricks with his pencils. I am better at climbing trees, so I show him the best branches. We also help each other with homework, though sometimes we laugh so hard we forget the math. On weekends we build forts from old boxes and invent silly games. We have different hobbies, but that makes our friendship stronger. When we disagree, we talk it out and shake hands. A good friend is someone who makes ordinary days feel special."},
  {"id":91,"level":"中级","title":"The Joy of Learning a Language","text":"Learning a new language opens doors that no key can turn. Every word you master is a small victory, and every sentence you form brings you closer to another culture. People often worry about making mistakes, but mistakes are part of the journey. A friendly smile and a few simple words can brighten a stranger's day in any country. The best way to learn is to practice a little every day. Read a short story, listen to music, or watch a film with subtitles. Over time, your confidence grows, and the language starts to feel like a second home. Do not wait for the perfect moment; begin today with one new word."},
  {"id":92,"level":"高级","title":"The Endless Mystery of the Night Sky","text":"Human curiosity has always reached for the stars, yet the night sky keeps most of its secrets hidden. Telescopes reveal distant galaxies whose light began its journey long before our planet existed, so that observing the cosmos is a kind of time travel. Astronomers study the faint echoes of ancient explosions and the slow dance of orbiting worlds, piecing together a story of creation that spans billions of years. The mathematics involved is elegant, yet the deepest questions remain humble ones. We ask whether we are alone, whether other planets harbor life, and whether the universe will expand forever. Each answer raises new riddles, and each generation adds another chapter to this endless inquiry. Science does not diminish wonder; it sharpens it."},
  {"id":93,"level":"初级","title":"My Morning Routine","text":"Every morning I wake up at seven o'clock. The sun comes through my window, and the birds sing outside. I stretch my arms and get out of bed. Then I wash my face and brush my teeth. For breakfast, I eat bread with eggs and drink a cup of milk. After that, I put on my clothes and take my bag. The bus comes at eight, and I sit by the window. I like to look at the trees and the shops as we pass. The ride is short, and soon I am at school. A good morning makes a good day."},
  {"id":94,"level":"中级","title":"What Travel Teaches Us","text":"Travel teaches lessons that no classroom can offer. When you step off a train in an unfamiliar city, your senses wake up. Strange smells drift from market stalls, unknown music plays in side streets, and the local language hums around you like a river. Getting lost is not a failure; it is an adventure in disguise. You might discover a quiet cafe, a hidden garden, or a street artist whose work moves you. Talking to locals with a mix of gestures and smiles often leads to the best memories. Keep a journal of small moments: the taste of street food, the warmth of the afternoon sun, the kindness of a stranger. These details become treasures you carry home, and they change how you see your own town."},
  {"id":95,"level":"高级","title":"History as a Living Conversation","text":"History is not a dusty shelf of dates but a living conversation between the past and the present. Every city street hides layers of older streets beneath it, and every custom we follow carries a forgotten origin. When we read an old letter or study a faded map, we touch the hopes and fears of people who lived through times of triumph and ruin. Historians remind us that progress is never a straight line; it bends, stalls, and sometimes retreats. Yet the record also shows remarkable resilience. Ordinary men and women have rebuilt their lives after wars, plagues, and disasters, often with little more than stubborn hope. Understanding their struggles does not change the past, but it sharpens our judgment about the present and warns us against repeating old errors."},
  {"id":96,"level":"初级","title":"My Friend the Cat","text":"I love animals very much. My favorite animal is the cat. Cats are soft and quiet, and they like to sleep in warm places. My cat is white with black spots. Her name is Mimi. In the morning, she walks to my bed and meows for food. I give her fish and milk. She is happy and rubs her head on my hand. In the afternoon, we play with a little ball. She runs and jumps, and I laugh. In the evening, she sits on my lap while I read. Animals are good friends. They do not need many words to show love. A small kindness to an animal brings a big joy to your heart."},
  {"id":97,"level":"中级","title":"Building Good Health Day by Day","text":"Good health is built from small daily choices rather than grand gestures. Sleep is the quiet foundation; adults need about seven to nine hours each night, yet many treat rest as a luxury. Regular exercise does not require a gym membership. A brisk walk after dinner, a few flights of stairs, or a short ride on a bicycle keeps the heart strong and the mind clear. Food matters too. A colorful plate of vegetables, fruit, whole grains, and lean protein gives the body the fuel it deserves, while sugary drinks offer only a brief spark. Water, sunlight, and time outdoors complete the picture. None of this demands perfection. A healthy life is simply a string of good-enough days, repeated with patience and kindness toward yourself."},
  {"id":98,"level":"高级","title":"Culture: The Air We Breathe","text":"Culture is the invisible air we breathe, the shared patterns of meaning that bind a community together. Music, food, festivals, and language are its visible signs, yet its true power lies beneath the surface. Every song carries the history of a people, and every recipe preserves the memory of a home. When cultures meet, they do not simply trade customs; they transform one another, borrowing a rhythm here and a flavor there until something new is born. This exchange can be uneasy, especially when power is unequal, and some traditions fade under pressure. Yet the human capacity to adapt is astonishing. Old tales find new voices, ancient instruments learn modern tunes, and young artists weave their heritage into fresh forms. To understand a culture, one must listen patiently and with an open heart."},
  {"id":99,"level":"初级","title":"A Fun Weekend with Friends","text":"On weekends, I like to do many fun things. In the morning, I ride my bike to the park. The trees are green, and the air smells fresh. I meet my friends there, and we play football together. After the game, we sit on the grass and share our snacks. Sometimes we draw pictures with chalk on the ground. My friend Lily draws a big sun, and I draw a little dog. We laugh a lot. In the afternoon, we go to the library. I borrow a book about space, and she reads a story about a brave girl. Reading is my favorite hobby. Friends make every day brighter, and I am thankful for mine."},
  {"id":100,"level":"中级","title":"The Rhythm of a City","text":"Cities have a rhythm of their own. In the early morning, the streets belong to bakers and bus drivers, while the first trains slide quietly through the tunnels. By midday, offices hum with the clatter of keyboards, and cafes fill with workers stealing a short break. The evening brings a different mood. Lights flicker on, shop windows glow, and people hurry home or meet friends for dinner. Working in a city means learning to move with the crowd, to wait patiently at crossings, and to find your own quiet corner in the noise. Some people find this pace exhausting; others find it thrilling. A good city, like a good job, offers both challenge and comfort, and it rewards those who pay attention to its small daily wonders."}
];

// 从文章池中随机抽取 n 篇（可用于刷新功能）
function shuffleArticles(n = 6) {
  const copy = [...ARTICLES];
  for (let i = copy.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
    [copy[i], copy[j]] = [copy[j], copy[i]];
  }
  return copy.slice(0, n);
}

window.ARTICLES = ARTICLES;
window.shuffleArticles = shuffleArticles;
